config: remember the stacked incrementals and USE of regenerate() per input

Sub-series: A (probe, match and config memos) - patch 5 of 10; applies to 3.0.83 with only the earlier patches of this sub-series.
Of these it needs none: it also applies to 3.0.83 by itself.
Ported to upstream 73646c35d: unchanged (line numbers only)

config.setcpv() calls regenerate() for every package, and regenerate()
stacks every incremental variable and then USE from the same values again
and again. The two stacking loops are moved, unchanged, into module
functions (_regenerate_stack_incremental, _regenerate_use_stack) that work
on the values regenerate() has read from its dbs, with the same lookups in
the same order. As functions of those values they can be answered from a
memo (4096 entries at most). A result is not remembered when the loop
printed a warning (a hit would not print it again) or, for USE, when a
"_*" wildcard made it depend on IUSE. regenerate() also records whether a
warning was ever printed (_regenerate_warned) and what its last call saw
(_regenerate_last); the two following patches read these.

On top of patches 0001-0011, resolve seconds before -> after
(median of the runs, at least 2 per figure):
  start 43.5 -> 41.8, holds 26.0 -> 24.1, masks 47.1 -> 45.2,
  py313 29.4 -> 27.2, bdeps 34.5 -> 31.9, policykit 25.9 -> 24.2
  sum of six 206.6 -> 194.4 (-12.2 s, -2.7 % of the stock sum 447.5)
Patches 0001-0012 together: 447.5 -> 194.4 s (-57 %); 447.5 is the
stock sum of the ladder run of 2026-11-20, stock was not run again here.

Weak spots and what was not checked:
* A memo hit skips nothing but the loops, but the functions must stay free
  of side effects other than the warnings. Memos live for the process
  (bounded by entry count, not bytes).

Origin: written by AI agents (Claude) in the emerge-fast job, 2026-11-17..20;
isolated from the full patch and measured on 2026-11-20..21. Not a human-written
change. Base: Portage 3.0.83. Test: `emerge -p -uND @world` on six states of
one system image, one resolve at a time; every run gave the same merge list
in the same order, the same number of backtracking attempts and the same
package count as unpatched 3.0.83.
